msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:61
msgid "2 ros2 service list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:63
msgid ""
"Running the ``ros2 service list`` command in a new terminal will return a "
"list of all the services currently active in the system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:65
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service list\n"
"/clear\n"
"/kill\n"
"/reset\n"
"/spawn\n"
"/teleop_turtle/describe_parameters\n"
"/teleop_turtle/get_parameter_types\n"
"/teleop_turtle/get_parameters\n"
"/teleop_turtle/list_parameters\n"
"/teleop_turtle/set_parameters\n"
"/teleop_turtle/set_parameters_atomically\n"
"/turtle1/set_pen\n"
"/turtle1/teleport_absolute\n"
"/turtle1/teleport_relative\n"
"/turtlesim/describe_parameters\n"
"/turtlesim/get_parameter_types\n"
"/turtlesim/get_parameters\n"
"/turtlesim/list_parameters\n"
"/turtlesim/set_parameters\n"
"/turtlesim/set_parameters_atomically"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:88
msgid ""
"You will see that both nodes have the same six services with ``parameters`` "
"in their names. Nearly every node in ROS 2 has these infrastructure services "
"that parameters are built off of. There will be more about parameters in the "
"next tutorial. In this tutorial, the parameter services will be omitted from "
"the discussion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:93
msgid ""
"For now, let's focus on the turtlesim-specific services, ``/clear``, ``/"
"kill``, ``/reset``, ``/spawn``, ``/turtle1/set_pen``, ``/turtle1/"
"teleport_absolute``, and ``/turtle1/teleport_relative``. You may recall "
"interacting with some of these services using rqt in the :doc:`Use "
"turtlesim, ros2, and rqt <../../../../../Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/"
"Introducing-Turtlesim>` tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:98
msgid "3 ros2 service type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:100
msgid ""
"Services have types that describe how the request and response data of a "
"service is structured. Service types are defined similarly to topic types, "
"except service types have two parts: one message for the request and another "
"for the response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:103
msgid "To find out the type of a service, use the command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:105
msgid "$ ros2 service type <service_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:109
msgid ""
"Let's take a look at turtlesim's ``/clear`` service. In a new terminal, "
"enter the command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:112
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service type /clear\n"
"std_srvs/srv/Empty"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:117
msgid ""
"The ``Empty`` type means the service call sends no data when making a "
"request and receives no data when receiving a response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:120
msgid "3.1 ros2 service list -t"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:122
msgid ""
"To see the types of all the active services at the same time, you can append "
"the ``--show-types`` option, abbreviated as ``-t``, to the ``list`` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:124
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service list -t\n"
"/clear [std_srvs/srv/Empty]\n"
"/kill [turtlesim_msgs/srv/Kill]\n"
"/reset [std_srvs/srv/Empty]\n"
"/spawn [turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn]\n"
"...\n"
"/turtle1/set_pen [turtlesim_msgs/srv/SetPen]\n"
"/turtle1/teleport_absolute [turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportAbsolute]\n"
"/turtle1/teleport_relative [turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportRelative]\n"
"..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:138
msgid "4 ros2 service info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:140
msgid "To see information of a particular service, use the command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:142
msgid "$ ros2 service info <service_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:146
msgid ""
"This returns the service type and the count of service clients and servers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:148
msgid ""
"For example, you can find the count of clients and servers for the ``/"
"clear`` service:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:150
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service info /clear\n"
"Type: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"Clients count: 0\n"
"Services count: 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:158
msgid "4.1 ros2 service info --verbose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:159
msgid ""
"For more detailed information about a service, you can append the ``--"
"verbose`` (or ``-v``) option to the ``info`` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:161
msgid "$ ros2 service info --verbose <service_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:165
msgid ""
"For example: you can get verbose information about the ``/clear`` service:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:167
msgid "$ ros2 service info --verbose /clear"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:171
msgid ""
"This will return additional information including the node name and "
"namespace of the service server, as well as the underlying middleware (RMW) "
"implementation details. An important information for Developers that the "
"``Endpoint count`` will be 2 for DDS based RMW implementations (connextdds, "
"cyclone, fastrtps) because DDS creates two endpoints (one for request and "
"one for response) for each service server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:174
msgid ""
"Type: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"Clients count: 0\n"
"Services count: 1\n"
"Node name: turtlesim\n"
"Node namespace: /\n"
"Service type: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"Service type hash: "
"RIHS01_5888399dedec5ccc85ea6451949fd2c9f97bfdf963f9a588821639fcd31b5d19\n"
"Endpoint type: SERVER\n"
"Endpoint count: 2\n"
"GIDs:\n"
"- Request Reader : 01.0f.93.f0.07.92.53.47.00.00.00.00.00.00.13.04\n"
"- Response Writer : 01.0f.93.f0.07.92.53.47.00.00.00.00.00.00.14.03\n"
"QoS profiles:\n"
"- Request Reader :\n"
"      Reliability: RELIABLE\n"
"      History (Depth): KEEP_LAST (10)\n"
"      Durability: VOLATILE\n"
"      Lifespan: Infinite\n"
"      Deadline: Infinite\n"
"      Liveliness: AUTOMATIC\n"
"      Liveliness lease duration: Infinite\n"
"- Response Writer :\n"
"      Reliability: RELIABLE\n"
"      History (Depth): KEEP_LAST (10)\n"
"      Durability: VOLATILE\n"
"      Lifespan: Infinite\n"
"      Deadline: Infinite\n"
"      Liveliness: AUTOMATIC\n"
"      Liveliness lease duration: Infinite"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:206
msgid ""
"Where as for non-DDS based RMW implementations like ``rmw_zenoh_cpp``, the "
"``Endpoint count`` will be 1 because it uses a single endpoint for both "
"request and response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:208
msgid ""
"Type: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"Clients count: 0\n"
"Services count: 1\n"
"Node name: turtlesim\n"
"Node namespace: /\n"
"Service type: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"Service type hash: "
"RIHS01_5888399dedec5ccc85ea6451949fd2c9f97bfdf963f9a588821639fcd31b5d19\n"
"Endpoint type: SERVER\n"
"Endpoint count: 1\n"
"GID: 59.b0.ea.78.57.3c.52.b4.c6.e9.af.44.22.3d.7c.f5\n"
"QoS profile:\n"
"  Reliability: RELIABLE\n"
"  History (Depth): KEEP_LAST (10)\n"
"  Durability: VOLATILE\n"
"  Lifespan: Infinite\n"
"  Deadline: Infinite\n"
"  Liveliness: AUTOMATIC\n"
"  Liveliness lease duration: Infinite"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:229
msgid ""
"If you want to learn more about different RMW implementations, refer to the :"
"doc:`About Different Middleware Vendors <../../../../client-libraries/About-"
"Different-Middleware-Vendors>` concept."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:232
msgid "5 ros2 service find"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:234
msgid ""
"If you want to find all the services of a specific type, you can use the "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:236
msgid "$ ros2 service find <type_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:240
msgid "For example, you can find all the ``Empty`` typed services like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:242
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service find std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"/clear\n"
"/reset"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:249
msgid "6 ros2 interface show"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:251
msgid ""
"You can call services from the command line, but first you need to know the "
"structure of the input arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:253
msgid "$ ros2 interface show <type_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:257
msgid "Try this on the ``/clear`` service's type, ``Empty``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:259
msgid ""
"$ ros2 interface show std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"---"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:264
msgid ""
"The ``---`` separates the request structure (above) from the response "
"structure (below). But, as you learned earlier, the ``Empty`` type doesn't "
"send or receive any data. So, naturally, its structure is blank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:268
msgid ""
"Let's introspect a service with a type that sends and receives data, like ``/"
"spawn``. From the results of ``ros2 service list -t``, we know ``/spawn``'s "
"type is ``turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:271
msgid ""
"To see the request and response arguments of the ``/spawn`` service, run the "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:273
msgid ""
"$ ros2 interface show turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn\n"
"float32 x\n"
"float32 y\n"
"float32 theta\n"
"string name # Optional.  A unique name will be created and returned if this "
"is empty\n"
"---\n"
"string name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:283
msgid ""
"The information above the ``---`` line tells us the arguments needed to call "
"``/spawn``. ``x``, ``y`` and ``theta`` determine the 2D pose of the spawned "
"turtle, and ``name`` is clearly optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:286
msgid ""
"The information below the line isn't something you need to know in this "
"case, but it can help you understand the data type of the response you get "
"from the call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:289
msgid "7 ros2 service call"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:291
msgid ""
"Now that you know what a service type is, how to find a service's type, and "
"how to find the structure of that type's arguments, you can call a service "
"using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:293
msgid "$ ros2 service call <service_name> <service_type> <arguments>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:297
msgid ""
"The ``<arguments>`` part is optional. For example, you know that ``Empty`` "
"typed services don't have any arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:300
msgid "$ ros2 service call /clear std_srvs/srv/Empty"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:304
msgid ""
"This command will clear the turtlesim window of any lines your turtle has "
"drawn."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:308
msgid ""
"Now let's spawn a new turtle by calling ``/spawn`` and setting arguments. "
"Input ``<arguments>`` in a service call from the command-line need to be in "
"YAML syntax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:311
msgid "Enter the command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:313
msgid ""
"$ ros2 service call /spawn turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn \"{x: 2, y: 2, theta: "
"0.2, name: ''}\"\n"
"requester: making request: turtlesim_msgs.srv.Spawn_Request(x=2.0, y=2.0, "
"theta=0.2, name='')\n"
"\n"
"response:\n"
"turtlesim_msgs.srv.Spawn_Response(name='turtle2')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:321
msgid ""
"You will get this method-style view of what's happening, and then the "
"service response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:323
msgid ""
"Your turtlesim window will update with the newly spawned turtle right away:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:328
msgid "8 ros2 service echo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:330
msgid ""
"To see the data communication between a service client and a service server "
"you can ``echo`` the service using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:332
msgid "$ ros2 service echo <service_name | service_type> <arguments>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:336
msgid ""
"``ros2 service echo`` depends on service introspection of a service client "
"and server, that is disabled by default. To enable it, users must call "
"``configure_introspection`` after creating a service client or server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:339
msgid ""
"Start up the ``introspection_client`` and ``introspection_service`` service "
"introspection demo."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:341
msgid "$ ros2 launch demo_nodes_cpp introspect_services_launch.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:345
msgid ""
"Open another terminal and run the following to enable service introspection "
"for ``introspection_client`` and ``introspection_service``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:347
msgid ""
"$ ros2 param set /introspection_service service_configure_introspection "
"contents\n"
"$ ros2 param set /introspection_client client_configure_introspection "
"contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/services/Working-with-services/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.rst:352
msgid ""
"Now we are able to see the service communication between "
"``introspection_client`` and ``introspection_service`` via ``ros2 service "
